  "strategy": "Approach and rationale: The objective is to minimize the maximum overlap integral between a step function h and its shifted complement 1-h. The previous algorithm relied heavily on local search and greedy heuristics, leading to suboptimal solutions. I propose a multi-resolution Bayesian optimization approach that balances global exploration with local refinement. This method explores low-resolution patterns first to find promising configurations, then refines them in higher resolutions. Bayesian optimization is more reliable for expensive-to-evaluate black-box functions with complex landscapes, automatically balancing exploitation (following the current best path) and exploration (trying new areas). The key innovation is using short-range patterns in the low-resolution phase to bootstrap the search in higher dimensions faster.\n\nMathematical specification: The optimization is over vectors h \u2208 [0,1]^n with \u2211h = n/2. The objective function is C5 = (max\u2096 \u222b h(t)(1-h(t+k))dt) * (2/n). The solution is represented as a compactly supported wavelet-like function with controlled fluctuations. We use constrained Bayesian optimization with Gaussian Process regression to model the objective function under the integral and sum constraints.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialize with parent solution (n=250) if available\n2. Phase 1: Low-resolution exploration (n=16) with Bayesian optimization:\n   - Define objective f(h) using the max overlap integral\n   - Constrained by 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and \u2211h = n/2\n   - Use expected improvement acquisition function\n3. Phase 2: Pattern extraction from local optimum\n   - Forward model the best pattern to higher resolution\n   - Preserve sum constraint during upsampling\n4. Phase 3: Local refinement with multi-start gradient descent\n5. Phase 4: Resolution scaling with linear interpolation and projection\n6. Phase 5: Exhaustive dropout search in active region\n\nThe candidate solution validation includes:\n- Meeting all constraints\n- Proper scaling of the candidate via interpolation\n- Time-outs detected via timer interrupts\n- Strict validation of the objective function\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Initial n=16 resolution\n- All-ones segment length variation\n- Noise parameter (0.01-0.1)\n- Temperature schedule (200 to 5 steps)\n- Dropout fraction (35%)\n- Local dim reduction (64%)\n\nEach variation affects computational efficiency and exploration range.\n\nComputational budget:\n- 5 seconds for low-resolution EBO\n- 60% time in local refinement\n- Dynamic budget allocation between resolution\n- Wall-clock timer checkpoints\n- 1100-second maximum observation\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Must return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- c5_bound computed with class_weights=[0.5]\n- Constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, \u2211h=n/2\n- Valgrind cleanup\n- Input gradient debugging\n- Failsafe fallback in case of error\n\nThe complete revamp uses adaptive pattern shifting and gradient-aware Bayesian optimization to escape local optima. The formulation preserves the integral constraint exactly via projection, not approximation. The high-dimensional projection step ensures global feasibility during error recovery.",
